{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>אִישׁ עַל דִּגְלוֹ בְאֹתֹת</b> (every man by his standard with signs) — Each standard had a colored cloth hung upon it; the color of this one was not like the color of that one. The color of each corresponded to the hue of its stone fixed in the breastplate, and through this each one recognized his standard — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 2:2). And Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra wrote (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 2:2): there were signs on every standard. And our predecessors said that on the standard of Reuben was the figure of a man, and depicted upon it were mandrakes (דוּדָאִים); and on the standard of Judah the figure of a lion, for with it Jacob compared him (Genesis 49:9); and on the standard of Ephraim the figure of an ox, because of \"his firstling ox\" (Deuteronomy 33:17); and on the standard of Dan the figure of an eagle, so that they resembled the cherubim that Ezekiel the prophet saw. And the Tent of Meeting was in the middle, and the camp of the Levites surrounded it within the camps, like the matter mentioned in Sefer Yetzirah, and the holy sanctuary is directed in the middle.\n\nNow the sides — their beginning is from the east, like the course of the sun, and after it the south, and after it the west, and after it the north. They set the standard of Judah in the east because it journeys first, as it is the leader, as HaShem commanded: \"Judah shall go up first\" (Judges 1:2 and ibid. 20:18), and Reuben in the south, in honor of the birthright. Now the two adjacent standards belonged to Leah, along with the firstborn of her handmaid born upon her knees, and one standard for the children of Rachel in the west, and the standard of the children of the handmaids in the north, last.\n\nFurthermore, I saw in the Midrash (Bemidbar Rabbah 2:9): Just as the Holy One, blessed be He, created four winds in the world, so He surrounded His throne with four living creatures, and above all of them the Throne of Glory, and corresponding to them the Holy One, blessed be He, arranged the standards for Moses. He said to him: East — from it light goes forth to the world — let Judah be opposite it, for he is the master of kingship; and with him the tribe of Issachar, who is the master of Torah; and with him the tribe of Zebulun, who is the master of wealth, as it is written: \"Zebulun shall dwell at the shore of the sea\" (Genesis 49:13), as it says: \"for they suck the abundance of the seas\" (Deuteronomy 33:19). They shall journey first, as it is said: \"their king passed on before them, and YHWH at their head\" (Micah 2:13). South — dews of blessing and rains of blessing go forth from it to the world — let Reuben be opposite it, for he is the master of repentance, and repentance is a good attribute, and the mercies of the Holy One, blessed be He, come to the world at the time when they do repentance. And with him Gad, who is the master of might — Reuben in repentance and Gad in might, and Simeon in the middle to atone for him. And they shall journey second, for repentance is second to Torah. West — treasuries of snow and treasuries of hail and cold and heat, and corresponding to them Ephraim and Manasseh and Benjamin, and the Shekhinah is always in the west, in the territory of Benjamin, as it is said: \"Of Benjamin he said: the beloved of YHWH shall dwell securely beside Him\" (Deuteronomy 33:12). And they shall journey third — fitting for Torah and for repentance is might, so that a person may prevail through Torah and prevail over his evil inclination. North — from there darkness goes forth to the world, and opposite it the tribe of Dan, because he darkened the world with the idolatry that Jeroboam made, as it is said: \"and he placed the one in Dan\" (I Kings 12:29). And with him the tribe of Asher, to illuminate the darkness, as it is said: \"he dips his foot in oil\" (Deuteronomy 33:24). And with him the tribe of Naphtali, who is the master of blessing. \"They shall journey last,\" for whoever worships idolatry goes backward and not forward.\n\nAnd there they further expound there corresponding to four camps of angels, etc., there in the Midrash. All of it was with wisdom and glory and greatness for Israel, and therefore Scripture mentions everything in detail."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ר״א is resolved as Rabbi Abraham, referring to Ibn Ezra, whom Ramban names in the parenthetical citation that follows. I read לפאות as 'the sides' (i.e., the four sides of the camp); the text as given reads להפאות in the source, but the translation follows the contextual sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:29.828064716Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}